# Telecommunications & Networks, part 1 (use cases 1-20): network operations and self-healing, radio, core and capacity, customer care
# Vendor-neutral. Automated network or customer actions use pre-approved templates, a named human approves anything above a risk threshold, and subscriber content is never an input.
== network-self-healing-remediation-agent | Network Self-Healing Remediation Agent | llm-agents,time-series | seconds | staff-principal | a=0.5 k=60
obj: Detect a customer-impacting network fault, match it to an approved runbook, propose or apply a fix through the orchestrator and verify recovery, so time to restore falls without unsafe changes reaching the live network.
loss: \mathcal{L}=-\sum_t\log p_\theta(y_t\mid y_{<t},x)+\lambda\,\mathrm{BCE}(\hat{p},y)
con: Only pre-approved change templates can be executed and anything above a risk threshold needs human approval ; Every action has a rollback and an automatic stop if service KPIs worsen ; The agent cannot touch lawful-intercept or core security functions ; Plans and tool calls logged for audit ; Handles 3M alarms per day with a first proposal inside 20 s
sn: Scope | A catalogue of approved remediation templates with blast-radius limits | Free-form commands generated by the model | A model that can type any command can take down a region
sn: Trigger | A correlated incident with confirmed service impact | Every raw alarm | Alarm floods make the agent busy and the NOC blind
sn: Verification | KPI recovery measured after the action against a pre-action baseline | Action reported as successful | A command that returns OK can still leave the service degraded
sig: Correlated incident severity | number of impacted subscribers and services behind the alarm group | Targets the agent at real impact | Computed from topology at alarm time
sig: Runbook match score | similarity of incident symptoms to the symptoms recorded in each approved runbook | Chooses the template | Runbooks must be versioned with the topology they were written for
sig: Blast radius estimate | count of cells and customers reachable from the target element | Sets the approval tier | Use the live inventory not a stale snapshot
sig: Recent change proximity | changes applied to the element or neighbours in the last 24 hours | Links faults to changes | Change records must be timestamped when applied not when approved
noise: Alarm text wording | vendor phrasing differs for the same fault
noise: Operator on shift | does not change the right fix
noise: Ticket priority typed by hand | inconsistent across teams
base: A rules engine that maps correlated alarm patterns to runbooks and opens a ticket for an engineer, because it is auditable and shows how much remediation can be automated.
prod: A planner agent over a read-only tool set for topology, KPIs and runbooks and a separate executor limited to approved templates, with an approval gate by blast radius, a post-action KPI verifier and automatic rollback.
pipe: Alarm intake and correlation @ stream processor ; Incident enrichment @ topology and inventory lookup ; Runbook retrieval @ vector index over approved runbooks ; Plan proposal @ planner agent with tool use ; Approval gate @ risk tiers and maker-checker ; Execution @ orchestrator templates ; KPI verification and rollback @ closed-loop check ; Audit log @ lakehouse
edge: Alarm storm during a major outage | the agent proposes many overlapping fixes | Incident-level locking so one plan owns a topology slice at a time
edge: Stale inventory | the plan targets the wrong element | Inventory freshness check before any action and abstention when stale
edge: Fix works but KPI degrades elsewhere | a neighbour cell absorbs the traffic and fails | Verify neighbour KPIs and roll back automatically
off: Share of incidents with a correct runbook proposed ; Rollback correctness in replay ; Rate of proposals blocked by guardrails
on: Mean time to restore for covered fault classes ; Incidents resolved without escalation ; Change-induced outages
gap: Letting the model compose raw commands ; Counting alarm closure as recovery ; Training on tickets that record the fix not the cause

== alarm-correlation-root-cause-grouping | Alarm Correlation & Root-Cause Grouping | graph,time-series | seconds | advanced | a=2 k=3
obj: Group the alarms raised by one underlying fault across a multi-vendor network and rank the likely root element, so the NOC works one incident instead of thousands of alarms.
loss: \mathcal{L}=\mathrm{BCE}(y,p)+\lambda\,\mathrm{GraphReg}
con: Grouping must respect the live topology and service dependencies ; A suspected root cause is a ranked suggestion never an automatic action ; Late alarms must join an existing incident ; Handles 5M alarms per day within 800 ms per alarm
sn: Structure | Topology and service dependency edges between elements | Time proximity alone | Unrelated faults in the same minute get merged
sn: Direction | Upstream elements raise root-cause candidates before downstream symptoms | Treating all alarms as equals | The loudest alarm is rarely the cause
sn: Memory | Historical incident groupings confirmed by engineers | Raw alarm counts | Counts reward noisy devices
sig: Topological distance to the alarm cluster | hop count on the dependency graph | Near the centre suggests the cause | Topology must be current
sig: Alarm onset order | which alarm in the group fired first within clock tolerance | Cause precedes symptom | Element clocks drift and need normalising
sig: Historical co-occurrence | how often two alarm types appeared together in confirmed incidents | Learns real fault patterns | Use only confirmed incident labels
sig: Service impact breadth | subscribers or services behind the candidate element | Ranks what matters | Join to the live service model
noise: Alarm severity assigned by the vendor | scales differ across vendors
noise: Alarm text length | a formatting artefact
noise: Element age | weak link to this fault
base: Rule-based correlation on parent and child dependencies and a fixed time window, because it is the incumbent and sets the bar for reduction in alarm volume.
prod: A graph model over the topology with temporal attention on alarm sequences, producing incident groups and a ranked root-cause list, with human feedback on each grouping fed back as labels.
pipe: Alarm normalisation @ stream processor ; Topology graph build @ graph store ; Candidate grouping @ temporal graph model ; Root-cause ranking @ gradient boosting over graph features ; Incident creation @ ticketing integration ; Engineer feedback @ review console ; Model refresh @ weekly retrain
edge: Flapping link | alarms appear and clear repeatedly | Debounce and merge flaps into one incident with a count
edge: Maintenance window | planned work looks like a fault | Suppress using the change calendar and tag the group as planned
edge: Missing topology link | the model splits one fault into two incidents | Show low-confidence merge suggestions to engineers
off: Alarm reduction ratio at a fixed grouping purity ; Root cause in top three ; Misgrouping rate on confirmed incidents
on: Tickets per underlying fault ; Time to first correct diagnosis ; Engineer overrides
gap: Time-window correlation without topology ; Evaluating on closed tickets that were merged by hand ; Ignoring clock drift

== predictive-network-fault-detection | Predictive Network Fault Detection | time-series,tabular | batch | advanced | a=24 k=2
obj: Predict element failures and severe degradations hours to days ahead from performance counters and logs so engineers can intervene before customers are affected.
loss: \mathcal{L}=\mathrm{FL}(\hat{p},y)+\lambda\,\mathrm{ECE}(\hat{p})
con: Alerts need at least 12 hours of lead time for planned work ; Precision above 40% at the chosen threshold to avoid alert fatigue ; Per-vendor models must be explainable to engineers ; Scores for 2M elements refreshed every 6 hours
sn: Label | Confirmed failures with timestamped root cause | Any alarm after the fact | Alarms include false positives and cascading effects
sn: Window | Counters over a horizon matched to the failure mechanism | One fixed window for all equipment | Optical, power and software faults have different lead signals
sn: Population | Compare each element to similar peers on the same vendor and software | Global thresholds | Equipment ages and loads differ widely
sig: Counter drift against peers | z-score of error and retransmit counters versus similar elements | Early degradation signature | Peer groups must exclude the failing element
sig: Temperature and power trend | slope of temperature and supply voltage over 7 days | Hardware ageing signal | Needs sensor calibration per model
sig: Software version and recent restart count | version and number of resets in 30 days | Instability indicator | Version must be as at prediction time
sig: Traffic load percentile | busy-hour load relative to capacity | Overload precedes failure in some classes | Seasonality adjustment is required
noise: Element hostname | encodes site not health
noise: Technician name on last visit | no causal link
noise: Raw alarm count | dominated by chatty devices
base: Static thresholds on key counters per equipment class, because they are what the NOC already trusts and set the minimum to beat.
prod: Gradient boosting per equipment family over peer-normalised counters with survival-style horizons, calibrated risk scores and a per-element explanation shown in the work queue.
pipe: Counter ingestion @ time-series store ; Peer normalisation @ batch features ; Risk model @ gradient boosting ; Calibration @ isotonic regression ; Work queue ranking @ priority by impact ; Intervention tracking @ ticketing ; Outcome labelling @ nightly join
edge: New equipment type | no history exists | Fall back to rules and collect labels
edge: Intervention hides the label | repaired elements look healthy | Record interventions and train on pre-intervention windows
edge: Software upgrade changes counter meaning | scores shift overnight | Counter definitions versioned with the software release
off: Precision and recall at 12 and 48 hour horizons ; Lead time distribution ; Calibration by equipment family
on: Unplanned outage minutes ; Planned versus emergency repairs ; Alerts closed as no-fault
gap: Random split across time ; Using post-failure counters as features ; Mixing vendor counter semantics

== traffic-reroute-congestion-mitigation | Traffic Reroute & Congestion Mitigation | reinforcement-learning,time-series | seconds | staff-principal | a=0.2 k=40
obj: Recommend traffic engineering changes that relieve congested links and routes within safe limits, so peak-hour quality holds without manual rerouting.
loss: \mathcal{L}=\sum_t \mathrm{Huber}\!\left(y_t-\hat y_t\right)
con: Changes limited to approved traffic-engineering templates ; Every recommendation simulated against the topology before use ; Human approval for changes that touch more than a set share of capacity ; Recommendation within 30 s of a congestion trigger
sn: Objective | Utilisation headroom on the worst links after the change | Average utilisation | Averages hide the single saturated link
sn: Safety | Simulated impact on all paths including failure cases | Greedy local fix | A fix that moves congestion elsewhere is not a fix
sn: Feedback | Measured quality after the change | Assumed model outcome | Traffic matrices change as soon as paths change
sig: Link utilisation percentile | p95 utilisation over 5 minutes per link | Detects real congestion | Use matching windows with the SLA
sig: Demand matrix estimate | estimated ingress to egress demand from flow records | Basis for simulation | Estimated demand has error bars
sig: Path diversity | number of alternative paths with spare capacity | Limits the options | Needs live topology
sig: Latency and loss on alternatives | measured delay and loss per candidate path | Quality guard | Measure on recent data
noise: Customer name on the largest flow | does not change routing
noise: Day of month | weak effect on load
noise: Interface description text | free text
base: A rule that shifts traffic to the next-best path when utilisation passes a threshold, because it is simple and operators trust it.
prod: A constrained policy search over a network simulator using a learned demand model, producing ranked reroute plans with predicted utilisation and a safety check, executed only through approved templates.
pipe: Telemetry and flow ingestion @ streaming ; Demand estimation @ time-series model ; Topology and capacity model @ graph store ; Plan search @ simulator with policy search ; Safety and approval gate @ risk tiers ; Template execution @ traffic engineering controller ; Post-change validation @ KPI check
edge: Demand spike from a live event | the model underestimates demand | Use conservative upper quantiles and widen the headroom target
edge: Failure during a change | the plan assumed a healthy network | Simulate single failures and abort on topology change
edge: Oscillation | plans keep moving traffic back and forth | Hysteresis and a minimum dwell time per plan
off: Predicted versus measured utilisation error ; Share of plans passing simulated failure tests ; Regret against an oracle on replayed days
on: Minutes of congested links at peak ; Customer-impacting packet loss ; Manual reroutes
gap: Optimising mean utilisation ; Skipping failure simulation ; Applying plans without a dwell time

== noc-incident-triage-copilot | NOC Incident Triage Copilot | llm-agents,nlp | seconds | advanced | a=1 k=30
obj: Summarise an incident for NOC engineers, pull the relevant topology, change history, similar past incidents and runbook steps, and draft the next actions so triage time falls.
loss: \mathcal{L}=-\sum_t\log p_\theta(y_t\mid y_{<t},x)+\lambda\,\mathrm{BCE}(\hat{p},y)
con: Every statement links to the source record it came from ; The copilot proposes and the engineer decides ; No write access to network systems ; Summary available within 10 s of an incident opening
sn: Grounding | Retrieved incident, change and topology records with identifiers | Model recall of how the network works | A fluent wrong explanation is worse than none
sn: Scope | Read-only tools for the engineer's own incident | Free access to every system | Broad access turns a helper into a risk
sn: Memory | Closed incidents with confirmed cause | All tickets including noise | Many tickets record the symptom not the fix
sig: Incident similarity | embedding and attribute match to past confirmed incidents | Finds precedent | Index only confirmed causes
sig: Change adjacency | changes near the same elements and time | Prime suspect list | Needs applied timestamps
sig: Runbook coverage | whether an approved runbook matches the symptoms | Directs the engineer | Version runbooks with topology
sig: Customer impact count | subscribers and enterprise accounts affected | Sets priority | Computed from live services
noise: Engineer chat banter | not incident evidence
noise: Ticket age | not a diagnostic signal
noise: Copy-pasted logs | duplicates evidence
base: Keyword search over past tickets and runbooks, because it shows how much value retrieval alone gives engineers.
prod: A retrieval-grounded copilot with read-only tools for topology, change history and KPIs, structured summaries with citations and an engineer feedback button on every suggestion.
pipe: Incident intake @ ticketing events ; Evidence retrieval @ search and vector index ; Topology and KPI tools @ read-only APIs ; Draft summary and next steps @ LLM with citations ; Engineer review @ NOC console ; Feedback capture @ lakehouse ; Quality evaluation @ weekly replay
edge: Conflicting past incidents | two precedents suggest different fixes | Show both with confidence and the differences
edge: Missing logs | the summary overstates certainty | State the evidence gap and ask for the missing source
edge: Prompt injection in ticket text | text tries to redirect the tool calls | Treat ticket text as data with tool allow-lists
off: Citation precision and faithfulness against engineer review ; Next-step usefulness ratings ; Retrieval hit rate for confirmed causes
on: Time to first diagnosis ; Escalations to tier three ; Repeat incidents
gap: Evaluating fluency not faithfulness ; Letting the copilot execute commands ; Indexing unresolved tickets as truth

== network-change-risk-scoring | Network Change Risk Scoring | tabular | seconds | advanced | a=0.05 k=15
obj: Score each planned network change for the probability that it causes a customer-impacting incident, so reviewers focus approval time on risky changes and safe ones flow quickly.
loss: \mathcal{L}=\mathrm{BCE}(\hat{p},y)+\lambda\,\mathrm{ECE}(\hat{p})
con: Scores must be explainable to the change board ; The score informs approval and never replaces the board ; No use of the engineer's identity as a feature ; Score returned within 5 s of submission
sn: Label | Changes linked to an incident by evidence of causation | Any incident in the same window | Coincident incidents flood the label with noise
sn: Context | Change type, target criticality and timing together | Change type alone | Risk depends on where and when the change lands
sn: Fairness | Risk from the change not the person | Engineer history as a feature | It punishes people who take on hard changes
sig: Blast radius | customers and services behind the target | Higher reach raises risk | Use live inventory
sig: Change complexity | number of steps and elements touched in the plan | Complexity raises error odds | Parse from the structured plan
sig: Time to peak traffic | minutes between the window and the next busy hour | Late changes hit peak | Use local time
sig: Past failure rate of the template | incident rate of this change template over 12 months | Template quality | Exclude the change being scored
noise: Free-text description length | not risk
noise: Requester team size | weak link
noise: Ticket number | an identifier
base: A checklist-based risk matrix by change type and target criticality, because that is how boards decide today.
prod: Gradient boosting on structured change features with monotonic constraints on blast radius, calibrated probabilities and per-change reasons shown beside the approval request.
pipe: Change intake @ change-management tool ; Feature build @ inventory and change joins ; Risk model @ gradient boosting ; Calibration @ isotonic regression ; Reviewer display @ board console ; Post-change outcome @ incident join ; Model monitoring @ drift and calibration
edge: Emergency change | there is no time for a full review | A fast path that still records the score and the reason
edge: New template | the template has no incident history | Start from a type-level prior and flag as new
edge: Delayed incident | harm appears days later | Long labelling window with a provisional label
off: AUC and calibration error ; Recall of incident-causing changes at the review cut-off ; Score stability across releases
on: Change-induced incidents ; Median approval time for low-risk changes ; Changes rolled back
gap: Labelling by time overlap ; Using engineer identity ; Scoring after the change is applied

== intent-to-configuration-translation | Intent-to-Configuration Translation | llm-agents,nlp | seconds | staff-principal | a=0.05 k=25
obj: Translate an engineer's plain-language network intent into validated vendor configuration through a typed intermediate model, so routine changes are faster and never skip validation.
loss: \mathcal{L}=-\sum_t\log p_\theta(y_t\mid y_{<t},x)+\lambda\,\mathrm{BCE}(\hat{p},y)
con: The model produces an intent record never raw device commands ; Configuration rendered by deterministic templates and checked by a validator ; Human approval before any push to the live network ; Dry-run diff available within 15 s
sn: Interface | A typed intent schema validated before rendering | Free text turned straight into commands | Free text can hide a destructive command
sn: Validation | Syntax, policy and simulation checks before approval | A reviewer reading the diff alone | Humans miss subtle config errors at volume
sn: Reuse | Existing approved templates extended by intent | Newly generated templates | New templates need their own review
sig: Schema conformance | whether the intent fits the typed schema without free-form fields | Gate against bad output | Schema versions with the platform
sig: Policy check result | pass or fail of the guardrail rules for the target | Blocks unsafe intents | Rules owned by network security
sig: Diff size | lines of configuration changed against running config | Reviewer effort and risk | Compute against live state
sig: Simulation outcome | reachability and policy test results in the digital twin | Predicts effect | Twin must be current
noise: Engineer writing style | not intent
noise: Time of request | weak link
noise: Prompt length | an input artefact
base: A form-based change builder with fixed fields per template, because it gives the safest path and a baseline for time saved.
prod: A planner that extracts a typed intent from the request, template rendering and policy validation in code, a digital-twin test and an approval step, with the model never touching device credentials.
pipe: Request intake @ chat or ticket ; Intent extraction @ LLM with schema output ; Schema and policy validation @ rule engine ; Config rendering @ deterministic templates ; Twin simulation @ network digital twin ; Approval and push @ orchestrator ; Audit @ lakehouse
edge: Ambiguous request | two plausible targets | Ask a clarifying question before any rendering
edge: Intent outside the schema | request needs a new template | Route to a template author instead of improvising
edge: Twin out of date | simulation passes but live differs | Block push when twin freshness exceeds a limit
off: Intent extraction accuracy on reviewed requests ; Validator catch rate on seeded bad intents ; Dry-run diff correctness
on: Lead time for routine changes ; Post-change defects ; Share of changes through the typed path
gap: Letting the model write commands ; Skipping the twin ; Measuring speed without defect rate

== cell-level-traffic-forecasting | Cell-Level Traffic Forecasting | time-series | batch | advanced | a=96 k=1
obj: Forecast traffic and user counts per cell for hours to weeks ahead so capacity, energy and maintenance plans use realistic demand with uncertainty.
loss: \mathcal{L}=\sum_t \mathrm{Huber}\!\left(y_t-\hat y_t\right)
con: Quantile forecasts for 200K cells ; Forecast refreshed every hour for the next 7 days ; Special events handled from a calendar ; Error reported per cell class
sn: Seasonality | Daily and weekly patterns with local holidays and events | A single global seasonality | Events shift demand by multiples
sn: Hierarchy | Cell forecasts that reconcile to site and region totals | Independent per-cell forecasts | Totals that do not add up break planning
sn: Censoring | Demand estimated where the cell was saturated | Observed throughput only | Saturated cells hide true demand
sig: Same hour last week | traffic at the same hour in the previous weeks | Strong seasonal anchor | Align on local time
sig: Event calendar flags | stadium, festival and holiday indicators near the cell | Explains spikes | Calendar must be curated
sig: Neighbour cell load | load on adjacent cells over the previous hour | Captures spillover | Beware of cells that go offline
sig: Recent trend | 14-day slope in busy-hour traffic | Growth signal | Dampen the trend to avoid runaway
noise: Cell name | an identifier
noise: Vendor model number | weak effect
noise: Hour of installation | no demand effect
base: Seasonal naive using the same hour in the previous week scaled by recent growth, because it is the easiest to explain.
prod: A global gradient boosting or neural time-series model with cell embeddings and calendar covariates, quantile outputs and hierarchical reconciliation across cell, site and region.
pipe: Counter ingestion @ time-series store ; Cleaning and censoring fix @ batch jobs ; Feature build @ calendar and neighbour joins ; Quantile forecast @ global model ; Hierarchical reconciliation @ batch step ; Forecast publish @ planning feed ; Accuracy tracking @ weekly report
edge: New cell | the cell has no traffic history | Borrow a profile from similar cells and widen the interval
edge: Cell outage | traffic drops to zero | Flag as outage not demand and exclude from training
edge: One-off event | there is no precedent to learn from | Event uplift priors and a manual override
off: Weighted quantile loss by cell class ; Coverage of eighty percent prediction intervals ; Bias at busy hour
on: Capacity upgrades triggered by false growth ; Congested cell-hours ; Energy savings from accurate low-load windows
gap: Training on saturated throughput as demand ; Ignoring hierarchy ; Splitting randomly across time

== ran-energy-saving-cell-sleep-optimisation | RAN Energy Saving & Cell Sleep Optimisation | reinforcement-learning,time-series | seconds | staff-principal | a=0.5 k=10
obj: Decide when to put cells, carriers and radio components into low-power states while holding coverage and user quality, so energy cost falls without degrading experience.
loss: \mathcal{L}=\sum_t \mathrm{Huber}\!\left(y_t-\hat y_t\right)
con: Quality guardrails on throughput, accessibility and drop rate ; Coverage cells never sleep and emergency services stay reachable ; Every state change reversible within seconds ; Policy decisions every 15 minutes for 200K cells
sn: Objective | Energy saved subject to quality constraints | Energy saved alone | Savings that hurt experience raise churn and complaints
sn: Constraint | Neighbour cells able to take the load | Sleep when load is low | Low load on one cell can mean a coverage gap
sn: Evaluation | Matched control cells and counterfactual estimates | Before and after averages | Weather and events confound simple comparisons
sig: Predicted load next hour | forecast of users and traffic for the cell | Safe sleep needs low demand | Forecast uncertainty must be used
sig: Neighbour headroom | spare capacity on overlapping cells | Absorbs the traffic | Needs live status
sig: Coverage criticality | share of area covered only by this cell | Prevents holes | Use planning data
sig: Wake-up latency | time to bring the cell back | Costs of a wrong sleep | Hardware specific
noise: Cell name | an identifier
noise: Weekday number alone | captured by load forecast
noise: Operator on duty | unrelated
base: A static schedule that sleeps capacity layers at night where load is below a fixed threshold, because it is already deployed and sets the savings floor.
prod: A contextual bandit or constrained policy over load forecasts and neighbour headroom with hard guardrails, shadow mode first, matched-control evaluation and an instant revert on KPI breach.
pipe: KPI and energy ingestion @ streaming ; Load forecast @ time-series model ; Candidate set and guardrails @ rule layer ; Policy selection @ constrained bandit ; Action push @ RAN controller ; Quality monitor and revert @ closed-loop ; Savings attribution @ matched controls
edge: Unexpected event | crowd arrives at a sleeping cell | Fast wake-up trigger on load and a conservative forecast quantile
edge: Neighbour failure | the backup cell is down | Check neighbour health before every sleep decision
edge: Seasonal drift | the schedule no longer matches demand | Weekly policy refresh with shadow comparison
off: Energy saved against matched controls ; Guardrail breach rate ; Forecast quantile coverage
on: Energy cost per site ; Customer quality complaints in sleep areas ; Wake-up events
gap: Counting before and after savings ; Ignoring coverage cells ; Skipping shadow mode

== sleeping-cell-detection | Sleeping Cell Detection | time-series | seconds | advanced | a=96 k=1
obj: Detect cells that appear healthy to the alarm system but carry no traffic or serve users badly, so silent outages are found in minutes not days.
loss: \mathcal{L}=\mathrm{FL}(\hat{p},y)+\lambda\,\mathrm{ECE}(\hat{p})
con: No dependence on alarms alone ; False alarm rate low enough for dispatch ; Distinguish planned downtime ; Evaluate 200K cells every 15 minutes
sn: Reference | Expected traffic from similar cells at the same time | A fixed minimum traffic | Quiet rural cells would trigger constantly
sn: Evidence | Several KPIs falling together | One counter | A single counter can be a measurement fault
sn: Labels | Engineer-confirmed silent outages | Cells that later raised an alarm | Alarm-labelled data misses the silent cases
sig: Traffic deviation from peers | ratio of actual to predicted traffic from similar cells | Primary silent signal | Peers from the same area and class
sig: Attach and handover success | success rate of connection attempts and handovers | Shows users failing | Needs sufficient attempts
sig: Neighbour load increase | extra load on neighbours when a cell goes quiet | Corroborates the silence | Verify neighbours are healthy
sig: Time since last user connection | minutes without any attach | Hard evidence | Zero in low-traffic cells at night
noise: Alarm count | the failure is silent
noise: Software version alone | weak link
noise: Cell age | unrelated
base: A threshold on traffic below a fraction of the cell's rolling median, because it is simple and operators already use it.
prod: A peer-conditioned residual detector with a classifier over KPI patterns, scored every 15 minutes and routed with the topology so field and remote teams see the suspected cause.
pipe: KPI ingestion @ time-series store ; Peer prediction @ forecasting model ; Residual and KPI features @ batch features ; Silent-outage classifier @ gradient boosting ; Planned-work filter @ change calendar ; Dispatch and remote reset queue @ ticketing ; Outcome feedback @ weekly labels
edge: Low-traffic hours | normal traffic looks like an outage | Scale scores by expected traffic and require several KPIs
edge: Backhaul fault | the cell is fine but unreachable | Check reachability and separate transport faults
edge: Event cancelled | expected crowd never arrives | Compare to peers not to the event forecast
off: Precision and recall on confirmed silent outages ; Time to detect against later alarms ; False alarms per 1000 cell-days
on: Silent outage duration ; Customer complaints per silent outage ; Truck rolls avoided
gap: Training only on alarmed cells ; Fixed traffic floors ; Ignoring planned work

== coverage-hole-detection | Coverage Hole Detection from Crowd Measurements | tabular,graph | batch | advanced | a=0.5 k=2
obj: Find locations with weak or absent coverage from anonymised device measurements and drive-test data, so planning targets the areas that users actually struggle in.
loss: \mathcal{L}=\mathrm{BCE}(\hat{p},y)+\lambda\,\mathrm{ECE}(\hat{p})
con: Device measurements aggregated and k-anonymised before analysis ; No individual subscriber location history stored ; Grid resolution of 50 m in dense areas ; Weekly refresh for all regions
sn: Sampling | Measurements weighted by population and activity | Raw sample counts | Samples cluster where people stand not where they need coverage
sn: Context | Indoor versus outdoor and device type | Pooled signal strength | Indoor losses are 10 to 20 dB
sn: Privacy | Aggregates with minimum counts | Individual traces | Traces re-identify people
sig: Weak-signal share | share of measurements below the coverage threshold per grid | Primary hole signal | Needs enough samples per tile
sig: Call setup failure and drop rate | failure rate for sessions in the tile | Experience evidence | Attribute to coverage not core faults
sig: Distance to nearest serving site | metres to the closest site | Explains the cause | From site inventory
sig: Terrain and building clutter | height and land-use class of the tile | Explains shadowing | Needs a recent map
noise: Individual device identifiers | privacy risk and no gain
noise: Handset brand alone | confounded with price
noise: Time of day alone | does not explain coverage
base: A map of signal strength percentiles from drive tests and sampled measurements, because planners already use it.
prod: A spatial model that estimates a coverage experience score per tile from aggregated crowd data and propagation features, with a ranked list of hole clusters and a suggested class of fix.
pipe: Measurement aggregation @ privacy-preserving batch ; Grid build @ geospatial index ; Propagation and clutter features @ geospatial join ; Coverage score model @ gradient boosting ; Hole clustering @ spatial clustering ; Planning list @ planning tool ; Validation drive test @ field verification
edge: Sparse area | too few samples to score | Report unknown not good and schedule a drive test
edge: New site switched on | old scores persist | Reset affected tiles on site activation
edge: Seasonal foliage | signal changes by season | Seasonal features and a rolling window
off: Agreement with drive-test confirmation ; Calibration of the weak-signal share ; Spatial AUC held out by region
on: Planned sites placed in confirmed holes ; Coverage complaints per area ; Drive-test cost
gap: Treating sample counts as demand ; Using individual traces ; Random splits that leak neighbouring tiles

== capacity-expansion-site-prioritisation | Capacity Expansion & Site Prioritisation | reinforcement-learning,tabular | batch | advanced | a=0.01 k=20
obj: Choose which sites and carriers to upgrade within a capital budget by predicted congestion, revenue and quality impact so spend goes where users benefit most.
loss: \mathcal{L}=\sum_t \mathrm{Huber}\!\left(y_t-\hat y_t\right)
con: Budget and vendor lead-time constraints respected ; Ranking explains the demand and quality drivers per site ; Planners can override with a reason ; Quarterly plan for 100K sites
sn: Demand | Forecast busy-hour demand with uncertainty | Last year's peak | Growth is uneven and events distort peaks
sn: Benefit | Estimated lift in user experience and retention from the upgrade | Utilisation alone | High utilisation with happy users is not urgent
sn: Constraint | Feasibility from power, backhaul and permits | Ranking that ignores delivery | A top-ranked site that cannot be built wastes the cycle
sig: Forecast busy-hour utilisation | forecast p95 utilisation at 6 and 12 months | Congestion risk | Quantiles from the traffic forecast
sig: Users affected | busy-hour users served by the cell | Scale of benefit | Exclude test devices
sig: Experience gap | share of sessions below the quality target | Evidence of pain | Match the quality definition across vendors
sig: Delivery feasibility | power, backhaul and permit readiness flags | Practical cost | From the planning system
noise: Site name | an identifier
noise: Last upgrade engineer | no link to need
noise: Vendor brand alone | confounded
base: Rank sites by forecast utilisation above a threshold within budget, because planners recognise the method.
prod: A constrained optimiser over predicted benefit per cost with scenario analysis, giving a ranked plan with reasons and sensitivity to demand growth, reviewed by planners.
pipe: Demand forecast @ time-series model ; Benefit estimate @ gradient boosting ; Feasibility join @ planning system ; Portfolio optimiser @ integer programming ; Scenario analysis @ batch simulation ; Planner review @ planning console ; Outcome tracking @ post-upgrade KPI check
edge: Demand forecast miss | a site fills faster than expected | Quarterly re-ranking and a watch list
edge: Vendor delay | the delivery plan slips by months | Re-optimise with updated lead times
edge: Political site pressure | overrides conflict with the ranking | Record overrides with reasons and review their outcome
off: Rank correlation with realised congestion ; Benefit estimate error after upgrade ; Optimiser gap to the best plan found offline
on: Congested cell-hours after upgrade ; Capital per resolved congestion hour ; Upgrades with no measurable benefit
gap: Ranking by utilisation alone ; Ignoring feasibility ; Not measuring the upgrade effect

== core-signalling-storm-detection | Core Signalling Storm Detection | time-series | sub-100ms | advanced | a=100 k=1
obj: Detect abnormal signalling surges in the mobile core early enough to throttle or isolate the source before it overloads authentication and session functions.
loss: \mathcal{L}=\mathrm{FL}(\hat{p},y)+\lambda\,\mathrm{ECE}(\hat{p})
con: Detection decision under 80 ms per window ; Throttling limited to approved templates with automatic expiry ; Emergency calls and priority traffic never throttled ; 50K signalling events per second
sn: Baseline | Per-function and per-source rate baselines by time of day | One global threshold | Healthy peaks look like storms
sn: Source | Concentration of load on a few devices or an app | Total rate alone | Misbehaving apps and devices cause most storms
sn: Action | Throttle the source class not everyone | Cell or core-wide throttle | Broad throttles hurt innocent users
sig: Rate versus expected | ratio of signalling per minute to the time-adjusted baseline | Surge signal | Baseline per function
sig: Source concentration | share of events from the top 1% of sources | Targets the cause | Needs pseudonymous grouping
sig: Failure and retry ratio | ratio of rejects and retries to requests | Positive feedback loop sign | Tune per message type
sig: Message mix shift | change in the mix of attach, auth and session requests | Pinpoints the function | Normalise by day type
noise: Subscriber name | irrelevant and private
noise: Server hostname | encodes location only
noise: Raw event count without baseline | follows traffic
base: Static rate thresholds per function with an alarm to the core team, because they are simple and easy to audit.
prod: A streaming detector with seasonal baselines and source concentration features that proposes a class-based throttle template, gated by approval above a severity level and expiring automatically.
pipe: Signalling ingestion @ stream processor ; Windowed rates @ streaming aggregation ; Baseline and anomaly score @ seasonal model ; Source attribution @ top-k sketches ; Throttle proposal @ rule layer ; Approval and apply @ core controller ; Recovery check @ KPI monitor
edge: Firmware update storm | legitimate devices all reconnect | Event calendar for known mass updates and staged throttling
edge: Throttle blocks priority traffic | safety calls fail | Priority classes excluded in the template and tested
edge: Detector lag | the surge peaks before action | Edge detectors with a fast path
off: Detection delay and false alarm rate on replayed storms ; Priority traffic loss in tests ; Source attribution accuracy
on: Core overload minutes ; Storm-induced outages ; Throttle duration
gap: Global thresholds ; Throttling by cell instead of source ; Not testing emergency traffic

== predictive-customer-service-agent | Predictive Customer Service Agent | llm-agents,tabular | seconds | staff-principal | a=0.3 k=40
obj: Spot customers whose service is degrading, work out the cause from network and account data and act first with a fix, a credit or a message, so many problems are resolved before the customer contacts care.
loss: \mathcal{L}=-\sum_t\log p_\theta(y_t\mid y_{<t},x)+\lambda\,\mathrm{BCE}(\hat{p},y)
con: Actions limited to a catalogue of approved remedies with cost limits ; Credits above a threshold need human approval ; Customers can opt out of proactive contact ; All actions and reasons visible to care agents ; 2M subscriber evaluations per hour
sn: Trigger | Measured service degradation tied to the customer | Any low score | Low scores include people who simply use little
sn: Remedy | A fix matched to the cause with a cost cap | A generic apology credit | Credits train customers to complain and erode margin
sn: Respect | Consent and contact preferences honoured | Maximum outreach | Over-contact increases churn
sig: Experience score trend | change in per-customer throughput, drops and latency over 7 days | Early sign of harm | Join to the customer's serving cells
sig: Cause attribution | whether degradation links to a cell fault, device or plan limit | Chooses the remedy | Needs network and device data
sig: Prior contacts | contacts about the same issue in 30 days | Avoids repeats | From CRM as at action time
sig: Customer value tier | plan and tenure band | Sets cost cap | Use bands not exact revenue
noise: Customer name | irrelevant and private
noise: Handset colour | no link to service
noise: Time since last bill | weak link
base: A rules engine that triggers a message when a customer's cell has an open outage, because it is simple and shows the reach of a basic approach.
prod: An agent that reads the experience score, attributes the cause with read-only tools and chooses from approved remedies with cost limits, writing every action to the case with the reason and a human approval gate above thresholds.
pipe: Experience scoring @ batch and streaming features ; Cause attribution @ rules and gradient boosting ; Remedy selection @ agent with a remedy catalogue ; Cost and consent gate @ policy engine ; Action execution @ CRM and messaging tools ; Case note and audit @ CRM ; Outcome tracking @ contact join
edge: False degradation | measurement glitch looks like harm | Require corroboration from two signals before acting
edge: Customer already contacted | duplicate outreach | Check open cases and recent contacts first
edge: Remedy cost spike | a major outage triggers mass credits | Budget caps per incident with human review
off: Cause attribution accuracy on audited cases ; Remedy appropriateness rating ; Opt-out compliance rate
on: Contacts avoided per 1000 customers ; Credit cost per avoided contact ; Satisfaction after proactive contact
gap: Counting avoided calls without a control ; Unlimited credits ; Ignoring opt-outs

== care-assistant-knowledge-base | Customer Care Assistant over Knowledge Base | llm-agents,nlp | seconds | advanced | a=1 k=25
obj: Answer customer and agent questions about plans, devices, coverage and billing from the operator's knowledge base with citations and safe escalation.
loss: \mathcal{L}=-\sum_t\log p_\theta(y_t\mid y_{<t},x)+\lambda\,\mathrm{BCE}(\hat{p},y)
con: Answers cite the knowledge article and effective date ; Account actions not allowed in this use case ; Escalation to a human on low confidence ; Response within 4 s at 200K chats per day
sn: Source | The current approved knowledge article | Model memory of plans | Plans and prices change monthly
sn: Scope | Questions the knowledge base can answer | Open-domain chat | A confident wrong answer about a price is a complaint or a refund
sn: Freshness | Articles tagged with effective dates | A static index | Old promotions are quoted after they end
sig: Retrieval relevance | similarity between the question and top articles | Grounds the answer | Evaluate on real intents
sig: Article effective window | whether the article is currently valid | Prevents stale answers | Needs structured dates
sig: Customer plan context | plan and device with consent | Personalises the answer | Use only what the task needs
sig: Answer confidence | agreement among retrieved sources and the model | Escalation trigger | Calibrate on audited answers
noise: Customer greeting style | not intent
noise: Time of day | no link to the answer
noise: Chat length | not quality
base: Keyword search over the knowledge base with a ranked list of articles, because it shows how far retrieval alone goes.
prod: A retrieval-grounded assistant with article-level citations, effective-date filtering, plan-aware context and a confidence gate that escalates to a human agent with the conversation summary.
pipe: Question intake @ chat channel ; Intent and plan context @ CRM lookup ; Retrieval @ hybrid search ; Grounded answer @ LLM with citations ; Confidence and policy gate @ rules ; Human handoff @ agent desktop ; Feedback and article gaps @ analytics
edge: Conflicting articles | two sources disagree | Prefer the latest and flag the conflict to content owners
edge: Question about a competitor | no source exists | Decline and offer the care team
edge: Prompt injection | customer text tries to change instructions | Treat input as data and never follow embedded instructions
off: Faithfulness and citation precision on audited answers ; Containment with correct resolution ; Escalation recall for risky questions
on: Contacts resolved without an agent ; Repeat contacts ; Handle time on escalated chats
gap: Measuring containment without correctness ; Indexing expired promotions ; Letting the assistant change accounts

== call-reason-prediction | Call Reason Prediction | tabular | sub-100ms | foundational | a=0.2 k=5
obj: Predict why a customer is contacting care as they enter the queue so the right skill, content or self-service step is offered immediately.
loss: \mathcal{L}=\mathrm{CE}(\hat{y},y)+\lambda\,\mathrm{BCE}(\hat{p},y)
con: Prediction within 60 ms of the contact starting ; Top three reasons shown to the agent ; No sensitive inference such as health or religion ; Customer can always choose another reason
sn: Label | The reason the agent coded after resolution | Menu choice at entry | Menu choices are guesses
sn: Recency | Events from the last few days | Lifetime history | Reasons are driven by recent events
sn: Scope | A fixed reason taxonomy | Free-form reasons | Reports need a stable taxonomy
sig: Recent bill event | days since the last bill and a bill change flag | Billing reasons | As at contact time
sig: Open network incident near the customer | whether a fault is open near their serving cells | Service reasons | From live incidents
sig: Recent order or plan change | order status and days since change | Activation and plan reasons | From order management
sig: Prior contact reason | the previous coded reason in 30 days | Repeat contacts | Use coded not free text
noise: Customer name | irrelevant and private
noise: Language of the greeting | not the reason
noise: Handset model alone | weak link
base: The most frequent reason for the customer's segment and the day of week, because it is a strong baseline for routing.
prod: Gradient boosting on event features with calibrated class probabilities and a fallback to the menu choice, served from a low-latency feature store.
pipe: Contact start event @ telephony platform ; Feature fetch @ online feature store ; Reason model @ gradient boosting ; Calibration @ temperature scaling ; Routing and self-service hint @ contact centre platform ; Agent coding @ wrap-up ; Retrain @ weekly
edge: New product launch | new reasons appear | Allow an other bucket and monitor its growth
edge: Outage day | one reason dominates | Condition on live incidents so the model adapts
edge: Wrong prediction shown to agents | agents anchor on it | Show it as a suggestion with confidence
off: Top-one and top-three accuracy by reason ; Calibration error ; Latency at p99
on: Transfers per contact ; Time to first correct skill ; Self-service resolution
gap: Training on menu choices ; Using lifetime history only ; Forcing agents to follow the suggestion

== per-customer-network-experience-scoring | Per-Customer Network Experience Scoring | tabular,time-series | batch | advanced | a=2 k=10
obj: Compute a daily experience score per customer from the sessions they actually had, so operations and care see who is truly affected rather than which cells are busy.
loss: \mathcal{L}=\sum_t \mathrm{Huber}\!\left(y_t-\hat y_t\right)
con: Score based on aggregated session KPIs not content ; Customers with too few sessions get no score not a guess ; Score tracks survey satisfaction ; 20M subscribers scored per day
sn: Measure | Quality of sessions the customer used | Average cell KPIs | Cell averages hide the customers in the tail
sn: Weighting | Weights learned against satisfaction and churn | Hand-set weights | Weights drift as apps change
sn: Coverage | A reason when no score exists | Score from thin data | A shaky score drives wrong remedies
sig: Session success rate | share of sessions that completed | Basic quality | Define per service type
sig: Throughput versus plan | achieved throughput relative to the plan and time | Visible speed gap | Device limits matter
sig: Latency and jitter for interactive apps | median and p95 per app type | Video and gaming experience | Use app classes not contents
sig: Drop and failure events | calls dropped and failed attaches | Frustrating events | Count per usage hour
noise: Customer name | irrelevant and private
noise: Data volume alone | usage not quality
noise: Handset age | weak link
base: The average KPI of the cells where the customer spent time, because it is easy and a baseline for the learned score.
prod: A tabular model trained on survey scores and subsequent contacts, using per-customer session aggregates, with score bands and the top contributing issue for each customer.
pipe: Session KPI aggregation @ batch ; Customer feature build @ lakehouse ; Experience model @ gradient boosting ; Score banding and reason @ rules ; Publish to care and ops @ data product ; Survey feedback join @ weekly ; Retrain @ monthly
edge: Heavy traveller | sessions in many cells | Aggregate by experience not by cell
edge: Roaming visitor | only partial data exists for the visitor | Score separately with fewer features
edge: Device fault | poor quality due to the handset | Add device features and a device reason
off: Correlation with survey satisfaction ; Lift in predicting care contacts ; Calibration of the bands
on: Contacts per low-score customer ; Churn among low-score customers ; Reduction in unaddressed low scores
gap: Averaging KPIs by cell ; Hand-set weights never revisited ; Scoring customers with too few sessions

== proactive-outage-notification-credits | Proactive Outage Notification & Credits | tabular,nlp | seconds | advanced | a=0.2 k=8
obj: Notify affected customers of outages in their own channel with accurate restoration estimates and calculate goodwill credits within policy, so contacts during incidents fall.
loss: \mathcal{L}=\mathrm{BCE}(y,p)+\lambda\,\mathrm{Calibration}
con: Customer lists derived from verified service impact ; Messages reviewed from templates and never promise unverified times ; Credits follow a published policy and a cost cap ; 5M notifications within 10 minutes of an incident
sn: Reach | Customers who actually lost service | Everyone in the postcode | Over-notification causes alarm and churn
sn: Estimate | A restoration range with a confidence | A single optimistic time | Missed promises are the biggest complaint
sn: Policy | Credits computed from policy rules | Case-by-case goodwill | Inconsistent credits cause disputes
sig: Impact membership | verified link between the customer and the affected elements | Targets the message | Join to live topology
sig: Restoration estimate | predicted time to restore from the incident type and crew status | Message content | Calibrated range
sig: Contact preference | channel and quiet hours | Respect and effectiveness | From consent records
sig: Service importance | enterprise, emergency or medical flags held with consent | Priority handling | Use only declared flags
noise: Postcode alone | overreach
noise: Customer value tier | should not affect who is told
noise: Message length | not effectiveness
base: Send a generic SMS to every customer served by the affected cells, because it is the current practice and sets the reach baseline.
prod: Impact-verified customer lists, a calibrated restoration model, templated multilingual messages and a credit engine following policy, with a dashboard for the incident manager to approve broadcast and credit actions.
pipe: Incident impact set @ topology join ; Restoration estimator @ gradient boosting ; Message templating @ localised templates ; Consent and channel gate @ policy engine ; Notification send @ messaging platform ; Credit calculation @ rules engine ; Contact monitoring @ analytics
edge: Estimate wrong | restoration slips | Update the message with a new range and apologise once
edge: Partial restoration | some customers recover earlier | Send recovery messages per customer
edge: Mass credit exposure | credits exceed the budget | Incident-level cap with approval
off: Impacted-customer precision and recall ; Restoration estimate coverage ; Credit calculation accuracy
on: Care contacts during incidents ; Complaints about notification ; Credit cost per incident
gap: Notifying by postcode ; Promising a single time ; Unbounded credits

== contact-centre-call-summarisation | Contact-Centre Call Summarisation & Wrap-up | nlp | seconds | foundational | a=1 k=30
obj: Produce an accurate summary, reason code and next action for each care contact so agents spend less time on wrap-up and records are consistent.
loss: \mathcal{L}=-\sum_t\log p_\theta(y_t\mid y_{<t},x)+\lambda\,\mathrm{BCE}(\hat{p},y)
con: Summary generated from the transcript and customer record only ; Agent reviews and edits before saving ; No storage of payment card details ; Summary ready within 5 s of call end
sn: Faithfulness | A summary that only states what was said | A fluent invention | Invented promises create liabilities
sn: Structure | Fixed fields for reason, resolution and follow-up | A free paragraph | Structured fields feed analytics
sn: Privacy | Redacted sensitive tokens before the model | Raw transcript to the model | Card numbers and passwords leak
sig: Transcript coverage | share of audio transcribed with confidence | Quality gate | Low confidence needs review
sig: Reason code agreement | agreement between predicted and agent-coded reasons | Training signal | Use corrected codes
sig: Commitment detection | promises such as callbacks made in the call | Follow-up tasks | Verify with the agent
sig: Sentiment shift | change in tone across the call | Escalation hint | Not for agent scoring
noise: Call length | not quality
noise: Agent name | irrelevant
noise: Background noise level | an audio artefact
base: A template that fills customer and product fields and leaves the notes to the agent, because it shows how much time summarisation saves.
prod: ASR with redaction, a summariser that fills fixed fields with cited transcript spans, a commitment extractor and an agent review step that records edits for training.
pipe: Audio transcription @ ASR service ; Redaction @ pattern and model redaction ; Summary and fields @ LLM with schema ; Commitment extraction @ LLM ; Agent review @ agent desktop ; Save to CRM @ CRM API ; Edit feedback @ lakehouse
edge: Accent and noise | transcript errors | Show confidence and require review below a threshold
edge: Multiple topics | one call with several issues | Allow several reason codes
edge: Redaction miss | a card number appears | A second pass check and a block on saving
off: Faithfulness against agent-reviewed summaries ; Field accuracy ; Edit rate by agents
on: After-call work time ; Consistency of reason codes ; Follow-up commitments missed
gap: Scoring on fluency ; Skipping redaction ; Using summaries to score agents

== complaint-classification-escalation | Complaint Classification & Escalation | nlp | seconds | advanced | a=0.1 k=10
obj: Classify complaints by type and severity and route those with regulatory or vulnerability risk to specialist teams within the required time.
loss: \mathcal{L}=\mathrm{CE}(\hat{y},y)+\lambda\,\mathrm{BCE}(\hat{p},y)
con: Regulated complaint categories never auto-closed ; Vulnerable customer indicators routed to a person ; Classification explained to the handler ; Routing within 60 s of receipt
sn: Taxonomy | The regulator's complaint categories | A custom set | Reports need to match the regulator's definitions
sn: Severity | Impact on the customer and legal exposure | Words such as angry | Tone alone mislabels polite but serious cases
sn: Recall | Catching the rare serious complaint | Overall accuracy | A missed legal threat costs far more than a misrouted query
sig: Complaint text cues | phrases signalling service loss, billing error, mis-selling or threat | Category and severity | Needs multilingual coverage
sig: Customer history | prior complaints and open cases | Escalation cue | As at receipt time
sig: Declared vulnerability | consented vulnerability flags | Specialist routing | Use only declared flags
sig: Channel and regulator mention | whether the regulator or ombudsman is named | Mandatory handling | Rule layer first
noise: Customer tone alone | not severity
noise: Message length | not importance
noise: Time of day | no link
base: Keyword rules for the regulated categories and a queue by product, because they guarantee the mandatory cases are routed.
prod: Rules for mandatory cases first, then a multilingual classifier for category and severity with calibrated probabilities and an explanation, with every routing decision logged.
pipe: Complaint intake @ channels ; Rule layer @ mandatory keywords and flags ; Classifier @ multilingual transformer ; Severity and routing @ rules and thresholds ; Handler queue @ case system ; Handler feedback @ labels ; Regulator report @ batch
edge: Mixed language | classifier confusion | Per-language evaluation and abstain to a person
edge: Complaint hidden in a query | the customer does not call it a complaint | Detect dissatisfaction cues and ask
edge: New regulation | categories change | Taxonomy versioning and quick retraining
off: Recall at the severe class at fixed precision ; Category accuracy by language ; Time to correct queue
on: Regulator breaches ; Time to first response ; Complaint reopen rate
gap: Optimising accuracy not recall ; No rule layer for mandatory cases ; Ignoring language coverage
